## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical tenancy controls in a NoSQL store; checks how to enforce data isolation with security rules, IAM, and network boundaries; assesses ability to design automated containment.

## Key Concepts
- Firestore security rules and tenant-scoped paths
- Per-tenant service accounts with least privilege
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls
- CI/CD gate with synthetic tenants and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Firestore rules snippet (illustrative)
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /tenants/{tenantId}/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.tenants.contains(tenantId);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for potential rule bypasses in CI?
- What are the trade-offs of using per-tenant collections vs single collection with tenant_id field?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end thinking for tenant-scoped data retention across multiple services with auditability and rollback capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- Data tagging and lineage across Storage, BigQuery, and Pub/Sub
- Tenant-scoped retention policies, TTLs, and legal holds
- IAM Conditions, per-tenant service accounts, and KMS constraints
- Immutable logs and cryptographic attestations for verification
- Rollback procedures and audit verification planning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for tenant deletion
function deleteTenant(tenantId) {
  purgePubSub(tenantId);
  deleteStorageObjects(tenantId);
  dropBigQueryPartitions(tenantId);
  verifyDeletion(tenantId);
  generateAuditReport(tenantId);
}
```
